WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:03.890
- [Narrator] A Kodak moment was
once synonymous with photos.

00:00:03.890 --> 00:00:06.950
- [Narrator] The moments
of your life are priceless.

00:00:06.950 --> 00:00:09.670
Are you saving them on the right film?

00:00:09.670 --> 00:00:11.220
Kodak Gold.

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:13.660
- [Narrator] At its peak,
Eastman Kodak was the early

00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:16.800
20th century equivalent
of Google or Apple,

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:19.860
once possessing a near
monopoly in the film business.

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:22.850
But the company's days at
the top are now a memory.

00:00:22.850 --> 00:00:25.120
Since the digital revolution unspooled

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:27.160
the company's dominant position,

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:29.560
itt's released products
to try and keep up,

00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:32.630
some a far cry from cameras and film,

00:00:32.630 --> 00:00:35.700
and recently an announcement
to produce drug ingredients

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:38.700
sent it's stock price surging
and embroiled the company

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:41.730
in a controversy that has
thrust it back in the limelight,

00:00:41.730 --> 00:00:42.960
which has raised questions

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:45.450
amongst consumers and investors alike.

00:00:45.450 --> 00:00:48.163
Can the company create
another Kodak moment?

00:00:50.490 --> 00:00:53.860
George Eastman got into the
photography business in 1880

00:00:53.860 --> 00:00:55.640
and he pledged to make taking photos

00:00:55.640 --> 00:00:57.650
as convenient as the pencil.

00:00:57.650 --> 00:01:00.067
You can see that notion
in an early slogan,

00:01:00.067 --> 00:01:02.720
"you press the button, we do the rest".

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:07.380
In 1884 he patented a way of
packaging film in a roll form

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:10.850
and four years later introduced
the first Kodak camera.

00:01:10.850 --> 00:01:13.640
Then in 1889, Eastman began selling

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:16.460
the first commercial
transparent roll film,

00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:18.160
which made it possible for Thomas Edison

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:20.810
to develop the motion picture camera.

00:01:20.810 --> 00:01:23.170
- So it's a highly profitable business

00:01:23.170 --> 00:01:25.000
and you don't have a
whole lot of competitors.

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:27.220
That's basically the
business model right there.

00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:29.390
- [Narrator] The company
innovated photographic solutions

00:01:29.390 --> 00:01:32.040
for consumers and businesses alike.

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:35.220
For example, the Rekodak
was a 1920s camera

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:39.230
that allowed banks to photograph
checks to prevent fraud.

00:01:39.230 --> 00:01:42.680
George Eastman died in 1932,
but his namesake company

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:45.520
stayed focused on his vision
of turning photography

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:48.940
and then film into a
pastime for ordinary people.

00:01:48.940 --> 00:01:51.740
- They had this one product
that had a high level

00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:53.360
of profit margin that allowed them

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:55.320
to do a lots of other things.

00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:57.660
They would get into lens manufacturing.

00:01:57.660 --> 00:01:59.690
They would get into the copier business.

00:01:59.690 --> 00:02:02.110
I mean, they had lots
of different businesses,

00:02:02.110 --> 00:02:06.300
but the core of it really was
the profit margin from film.

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:07.670
- [Narrator] Kodak's dominance continued

00:02:07.670 --> 00:02:11.390
through World War II, then
competition began to emerge.

00:02:11.390 --> 00:02:15.410
Polaroid released in 1948 a
new camera that turned out

00:02:15.410 --> 00:02:19.110
a finished picture one minute
after the shutter snapped,

00:02:19.110 --> 00:02:22.470
but Kodak remained in the
eyes of some, a monopoly

00:02:22.470 --> 00:02:25.810
and faced multiple antitrust
cases from the US government.

00:02:25.810 --> 00:02:28.900
Still the company continued
introducing new products

00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:31.880
like simpler, more
affordable movie cameras.

00:02:31.880 --> 00:02:33.740
- Let me show you how
easy Kodak has made it

00:02:33.740 --> 00:02:36.810
for you and me with a Kodak
Instamatic movie camera.

00:02:36.810 --> 00:02:38.180
- [Narrator] And with an
eye towards maintaining

00:02:38.180 --> 00:02:40.010
it's position well into the future,

00:02:40.010 --> 00:02:44.360
the company in 1978 patented
the first digital camera,

00:02:44.360 --> 00:02:46.940
which saved images onto a cassette tape,

00:02:46.940 --> 00:02:50.250
but it was slow on commercializing
digital camera technology

00:02:50.250 --> 00:02:53.030
because it feared it would
cannibalize its film business.

00:02:53.030 --> 00:02:55.738
- Digital photography was
not their prime business.

00:02:55.738 --> 00:02:58.850
Digital photography was largely funded by

00:02:58.850 --> 00:03:00.660
the profits made by film.

00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:02.850
- [Narrator] By 1988,
Kodak had become a mammoth

00:03:02.850 --> 00:03:04.610
international company that employed

00:03:04.610 --> 00:03:07.440
more than 145,000 people.

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:10.040
That year it had its
highest earnings ever.

00:03:10.040 --> 00:03:13.410
The company viewed rival
Fuji as its biggest threat.

00:03:13.410 --> 00:03:16.020
The Japanese company was
going after Kodak loyalists

00:03:16.020 --> 00:03:18.130
with promotions and advertising.

00:03:18.130 --> 00:03:22.380
By 1989, Fuji had captured
11% of the US market.

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:24.620
For Kodak the days of double digit growth

00:03:24.620 --> 00:03:26.460
in its film business were over.

00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:30.430
In 1993 the film giant brought
in a CEO from the outside

00:03:30.430 --> 00:03:31.620
to turn things around.

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:34.950
George Fisher was credited
with Motorola's resurgence

00:03:34.950 --> 00:03:36.470
and when he got to Kodak,

00:03:36.470 --> 00:03:39.740
Fisher fought what he viewed
as Fuji's unfair advantage

00:03:39.740 --> 00:03:41.400
in the Japanese market.

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:43.950
- What we face is in
fact a highly controlled

00:03:43.950 --> 00:03:47.940
distribution system that
denies Kodak full access

00:03:47.940 --> 00:03:51.010
to Japan's consumer film and paper market.

00:03:51.010 --> 00:03:52.770
- [Narrator] The World Trade
Organization ultimately

00:03:52.770 --> 00:03:55.760
rejected US claims of
Japanese protectionism

00:03:55.760 --> 00:03:57.360
in photographic film.

00:03:57.360 --> 00:03:59.560
Meanwhile, Fisher pursued another priority

00:03:59.560 --> 00:04:02.250
pushing the company into the digital era.

00:04:02.250 --> 00:04:04.840
- But it wasn't a profitable
is to say probably

00:04:04.840 --> 00:04:07.040
they didn't make money
on any of these products.

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:09.380
- [Narrator] In 1997,
digital imaging could

00:04:09.380 --> 00:04:11.690
become a loss leader for the company.

00:04:11.690 --> 00:04:13.680
That year Kodak was on track to lose

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:18.250
an estimated $400 million and
in traditional film products,

00:04:18.250 --> 00:04:20.840
Fuji was continuing to gain market share.

00:04:20.840 --> 00:04:23.920
That fall Kodak slashed 10,000 jobs.

00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:26.560
At the turn of the millennium
a new CEO entered the scene

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:28.840
and introduced a slew of new products.

00:04:28.840 --> 00:04:30.720
Some of them actually took.

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:33.530
It entered the inkjet and
digital printer markets

00:04:33.530 --> 00:04:36.700
competing head on with Hewlett
Packard, Canon and Epson.

00:04:36.700 --> 00:04:39.850
- So they said, all right,
prints are the way to go.

00:04:39.850 --> 00:04:42.620
So we'll sell a home printer
for a reasonable money,

00:04:42.620 --> 00:04:44.920
but unfortunately the
one thing that happened

00:04:44.920 --> 00:04:47.330
is with this business is it
just never really took off

00:04:47.330 --> 00:04:48.580
because people just don't make

00:04:48.580 --> 00:04:50.430
that many photographic prints anymore.

00:04:50.430 --> 00:04:52.660
- [Narrator] People stopped
printing photos in part

00:04:52.660 --> 00:04:54.160
because this happened.

00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:57.750
- Every once in a while, a
revolutionary product comes along

00:04:58.660 --> 00:05:00.960
that changes everything.

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:04.050
- [Narrator] In 2007,
Apple's always with you cam

00:05:04.050 --> 00:05:08.020
the iPhone ushered in a
photographic revolution.

00:05:08.020 --> 00:05:09.520
- On the back, the biggest thing of note

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:11.920
is we got a two megapixel
camera built right in.

00:05:13.357 --> 00:05:17.520
- [Narrator] Between 2008 and
2011 Kodak's digital camera

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:20.366
sales began to decline year after year.

00:05:20.366 --> 00:05:22.250
To survive it sold off

00:05:22.250 --> 00:05:25.030
some of its businesses
and its patent portfolio.

00:05:25.030 --> 00:05:28.960
- If you look at sales of
point and shoot cameras,

00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:31.580
whether it's film cameras
or digital cameras,

00:05:31.580 --> 00:05:33.560
they're basically almost gone.

00:05:33.560 --> 00:05:35.220
- [Narrator] In 2012, the company filed

00:05:35.220 --> 00:05:36.790
for bankruptcy protection

00:05:36.790 --> 00:05:39.050
and got out of the
digital camera business.

00:05:39.050 --> 00:05:42.960
For a new generation iPhone
and Instagram supplanted Kodak

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:45.800
as the brand synonymous with photography.

00:05:45.800 --> 00:05:47.410
Kodak emerged from bankruptcy

00:05:47.410 --> 00:05:50.080
about a year later with a new CEO.

00:05:50.080 --> 00:05:53.590
- The Kodak brand is probably
one of the best known brand

00:05:53.590 --> 00:05:54.610
names in the world.

00:05:54.610 --> 00:05:58.100
So part of what they ended
up doing was to license

00:05:58.100 --> 00:05:59.810
that word, their name.

00:05:59.810 --> 00:06:02.060
You name it, they put their name on it.

00:06:02.060 --> 00:06:04.700
- [Narrator] The film company
pivoted to commercial printing

00:06:04.700 --> 00:06:07.440
and put its name on
tablets and smartphones.

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:10.520
In 2018, it introduced
its own cryptocurrency

00:06:10.520 --> 00:06:12.352
called KodakCoin as part of a platform

00:06:12.352 --> 00:06:16.100
to help photographers track
and license their work.

00:06:16.100 --> 00:06:17.240
But over the last decade,

00:06:17.240 --> 00:06:20.410
the company's stock price
has continued to decline,

00:06:20.410 --> 00:06:21.920
then this happened.

00:06:21.920 --> 00:06:23.730
- The coronavirus crisis.

00:06:23.730 --> 00:06:25.780
- [Reporter] The coronavirus
death toll rising.

00:06:25.780 --> 00:06:27.710
- And there are cases of the disease

00:06:27.710 --> 00:06:29.100
here in the United States.

00:06:29.100 --> 00:06:31.410
- [Narrator] In July amidst
concerns about availability

00:06:31.410 --> 00:06:34.420
of American products
to help in the pandemic

00:06:34.420 --> 00:06:37.300
President Donald Trump
announced Kodak had won

00:06:37.300 --> 00:06:41.448
a first of its kind $765
million government loan

00:06:41.448 --> 00:06:43.890
under the Defense Production Act.

00:06:43.890 --> 00:06:46.879
- My administration has
reached a historic agreement

00:06:46.879 --> 00:06:49.690
with a great American company.

00:06:49.690 --> 00:06:52.240
You remember this company it's called

00:06:52.240 --> 00:06:54.270
from the good old camera age,

00:06:54.270 --> 00:06:58.168
the old days to begin producing critical

00:06:58.168 --> 00:07:02.040
pharmaceutical ingredients,
it's called Kodak.

00:07:02.040 --> 00:07:04.212
- Kodak was a chemical company.

00:07:04.212 --> 00:07:07.780
They produced their own
processing chemicals

00:07:07.780 --> 00:07:10.720
that went with photography, for a while

00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:14.000
they own Sterling drug,
which is a chemical company.

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:16.610
So again, whether people
realize it or not,

00:07:16.610 --> 00:07:18.530
it actually makes sense
for them to do that.

00:07:18.530 --> 00:07:21.290
That's kind of what
their core business was.

00:07:21.290 --> 00:07:23.530
- [Narrator] The company's
chief executive Jim Continenza

00:07:23.530 --> 00:07:26.980
said he expected the loan to
create 300 jobs in Rochester

00:07:26.980 --> 00:07:30.850
where Kodak's based and was
confident it would go through.

00:07:30.850 --> 00:07:32.970
- Well, we feel very comfortable
that we can bank on it.

00:07:32.970 --> 00:07:34.680
- [Narrator] But it soon
became clear to investors

00:07:34.680 --> 00:07:37.150
that the deal was far from done.

00:07:37.150 --> 00:07:39.470
A misworded alert about
the loan that Kodak

00:07:39.470 --> 00:07:42.190
sent to local media before
the president's announcement

00:07:42.190 --> 00:07:45.560
sent the company's stock
price on a rollercoaster ride.

00:07:45.560 --> 00:07:48.010
The deal was put on pause as the SEC looks

00:07:48.010 --> 00:07:49.890
at how Kodak disclosed the loan

00:07:49.890 --> 00:07:52.110
and stock option grants to executives.

00:07:52.110 --> 00:07:54.757
All this happens as the
company is losing money,

00:07:54.757 --> 00:07:56.970
$5 million in the second quarter,

00:07:56.970 --> 00:08:00.920
compared with a profit of
$201 million a year ago.

00:08:00.920 --> 00:08:04.870
Kodak, a company whose products
helps capture era after era

00:08:04.870 --> 00:08:06.450
is still trying to redefine

00:08:06.450 --> 00:08:08.573
its own image in the current one.

